to an A button at  the lower left of the presentation  window doctor Pennington will answer as  many questions as time permits and the  first question is is it necessary to  measure multiple transitions per peptide  and also doesn't quantification work  with the most intense transition yes  thank you thank you for that question  and it's a good question so  quantification does work with the most  intense transition and indeed there are  a number of papers that show that if you  measure the intense intense transition  versus measuring the three highest  intensity transitions the quantification  data is very similar so quantification  is done with the most intense transition  however we measure multiple transitions  per peptide because those are the those  transitions and the rank order of the  intensity of those transitions allows us  to determine whether the peptide and the  fragments generated from it are specific  to the peptide that we're attempting to  measure so they provide if you like a  quality assessment that the transitions  that we're measuring do indeed come from  the peptide that we're attempting to  measure do you include isotope labeled  peptides for each peptide you're  measuring at the moment we don't and  there's and I think there's ongoing  debate about whether this is whether  this is really necessary in many  instances in equivalent scenarios where  people are measuring for example  multiple messenger RNAs using reverse  transcriptase PCR they don't absolutely  quantify each of those those transcripts  instead they measure the relative levels  of the transcripts and use an algorithm  to derive a score or a numerical value  for the combination of them and I think  that's something that we anticipate will  be done in a similar fashion here that  we will measure the relative amounts of  those peptides and then using the  algorithm that we've developed produce a  score and it  be necessary to analytically measure and  quantify each of the peptides and  therefore we don't include isotope  labeled peptides is the test specific to  prostate cancer have you tested it  against other cancers and again that's  an interesting question and it's one  that we've we've given a significant  consideration to because what the test  isn't is a test that's used for the  diagnosis of prostate cancer it's a test  that you that's used subsequent to a  gold standard diagnosis of prostate  cancer which is a tissue biopsy so we  know that the test is only going to be  used in patients who have a diagnosis of  prostate cancer and that's what we  haven't tested whether it's whether it  measures or it is interfered with by  other cancers because we're assuming  because our men have been the men that  have been diagnosed with prostate cancer  have been very carefully clinically  evaluated that these are men who have  prostate cancer and not other tumors  there's the test work on plasma again a  good question and it's and this really  revolves around the difference between  plasma and serum and the challenges in  in using both of those those blood  matrices at the moment we are beginning  a process to evaluate whether the test  works on plasma there's a priori no  reason why it shouldn't but four  considerations they probably don't have  time to go into our preferred matrix has  always been serum and that as with all  of these things when you choose  something it has advantages and  limitations and of course the biggest  limitation with serum is that it  requires a blood draw and then  subsequent centrifuge centrifugation  which limits then the point of use of  the test so in this case the sample  that's used for the test has to be taken  in a clinic that has access to the  ability to prepare serum and so for  example using serum means that it could  never be a test at the moment could  be a test that could be done with a  blood draw at home whereas there's a  possibility that that would be the case  if it worked on plasma so at the moment  we're evaluating its ability to work on  plasma because that would increase the  number of sites at which the blood could  be used drawn for the use for that for  use for the test how efficient is  expression of a particular biomarker in  diagnosis of a specific cancer and can a  single biomarker be found in different  type carcinomas so I suppose that that  goes back to my question about that  diagnosis and so what we specifically  haven't attempted to do here is to  identify a protein that could be used to  diagnose the cancer I personally think  it's highly unlikely that a single  biomarker will be found that's specific  for particular cancers and and that rage  means that that because there are a  whole series of considerations the  expectation would be that that that  protein would have to be cancer specific  it would have to be a protein that's  produced in the cancer is created or  finds its way into the blood and then  could be measured at the level required  to be able to detect the tumor at an  early stage and I think that that's a  tall order for a single protein it's  probably even a tall order raised for a  number of different proteins and of  course if you're going to evaluate then  whether the protein or proteins really  are specific for a particular cancer  then the evaluation studies that need to  be performed need to rule out the  possibility that that protein also  identifies patients with other tumors  and so the samples then need to come  from patients who've got a range of  different tumors as well as obviously  healthy normal controls and that means  that the whole process of identifying a  protein that's a diagnostic biomarker  for a particular cancer is significantly  more challenging as we were waiting for  additional questions to come in I would  like to once again thank dr. Pennington  for his presentation  do you have any final comments sorry do  I have any final comments you'd like to  share and I suppose my final thought  that I'd a couple of thoughts the first  is that that I think it's becoming  apparent that these multiplexed mrm  measurements are being converted into  tests that's occurring as with many of  these molecular tests most most advances  being made in the area of cancer and so  there are mrm tests being developed for  lung cancer the prospect of these  multiplex tests being used as  laboratory-developed tests I think has  been improved significantly by a recent  ruling from the FDA saying that  multiplex mass spectrometry based tests  will be subject to phytate 510k  exemption and so that means that for the  regulatory process they can be advanced  and so I think that that the interest in  developing multiplex  tests for cancers is significant in our  own case we're also developing these as  alluded to previously in ceramic  arthritis and inflammatory conditions  and a number of other disease states so  I think they hold enormous potential for  the future thank you I'd like to thank  the audience for joining us today and  for their interesting questions  questions we did not have time for today  will be addressed by the speaker via the  contact information you provided at the  time of registration we would like to  thank our educational sponsor agilent  technologies for today's webcast this  webcast can be viewed on-demand through  February 2018 lab routes will alert you  via email when it's available for replay  and we encourage you to share that email  with your colleagues who may have missed  today's live event until next time  goodbye
 

